It is established in Theorem III.2.4 that when \(\mathbf{f}\!\in\!L^{r}(\Omega)^{2}\), \(\operatorname{curl}\mathbf{u}\!\in\!W^{1,r}(\Omega)\) and \(p\!\in\!W^{1,r}(\Omega)\) then the Stokes Problem (4.8) is equivalent to:

_Find \(\psi\!\in\!\varPhi_{s}\) and \(\omega\!\in\!W^{1,r}(\Omega)\) such that:_

(4.28a) \[\begin{cases}(\operatorname{\mathbf{curl}}\omega,\operatorname{\mathbf{ curl}}\phi)=(\mathbf{f},\operatorname{\mathbf{curl}}\phi)&\quad\forall\phi\! \in\!\varPhi_{s},\\ (\operatorname{\mathbf{curl}}\psi,\operatorname{\mathbf{curl}}\mu)=(\omega, \mu)&\quad\forall\mu\!\in\!W^{1,r}(\Omega),\end{cases}\]

_Find \(p\!\in\!W^{1,r}(\Omega)\cap L^{2}_{0}(\Omega)\) such that:_

(4.28b) \[(\operatorname{\mathbf{grad}}p,\operatorname{\mathbf{grad}}q)=(\mathbf{f}- \operatorname{\mathbf{curl}}\omega,\operatorname{\mathbf{grad}}q)\quad\quad \forall q\!\in\!W^{1,s}(\Omega),\]

_with \(\mathbf{u}=\operatorname{\mathbf{curl}}\psi\) and \(\omega=\operatorname{curl}\mathbf{u}\)._

